course: dictionary
lesson: 2928
title: Castle words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A drawbridge is a bridge that can be pulled up or lifted. You can work this out from the words around it. «Toad is dragged off in chains, shrieking, across the market-place... over a drawbridge into a grim old castle». Toad goes over it into the castle, so a drawbridge is a bridge that leads into a castle.
explain: Castle stories give more clues. The first castles were wooden towers on earth mounds; later castles were built of stone, with thick walls, towers, moats and drawbridges. A moat is a deep ditch around a castle, and together with the drawbridge it helps keep enemies out.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «over a bridge that can be lifted into a grim old castle». It still makes sense, so your meaning fits. Then compare with the other meaning: Tower Bridge in London is a modern bridge that lifts to let tall ships pass. It works like a drawbridge, but its job is to let ships through, not to keep enemies out.
fact: A drawbridge is a bridge that can be pulled up or lifted.
fact: «Raise the drawbridge!» means the castle is closing to attackers.
fact: A drawbridge is one of a castle's main defences, along with the moat, portcullis and battlements.
fact: «Portcullis» means a heavy gate that drops down.
fact: Tower Bridge in London is a famous modern bridge that lifts to let tall ships pass.
exercise: Toad goes «over a drawbridge into a grim old castle». What is a drawbridge? | A tall tower with a flag on top | A deep hole dug for treasure | *A bridge that can be pulled up or lifted
exercise: «Raise the drawbridge!» shouts the guard. What does this mean? | The moat has dried up | *The castle is closing to attackers | The castle is opening its gates to visitors | The guard wants a drink of water
exercise: Tower Bridge in London lifts up. What is it there for? | To keep dragons out of the city | To give the castle guards a rest | *To let tall ships pass underneath
test: Find the meaning of «portcullis» in «the spiky portcullis». What is a portcullis? | Bright red armour | A bird that lives in castles | *A heavy gate that drops down | A wide, flat boat
test: Why would a castle lord raise the drawbridge? | To dry out the moat | To let the cows graze in the moat | To make the castle look bigger | *To keep attackers out
exam: A bridge that lifts to let tall ships pass, like Tower Bridge in London, is called a what? | *drawbridge | portcullis | moat | castle
